  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe optimization problem for minimizing C\u2085 is non-convex and challenging due to its combinatorial nature. We propose a hybrid approach combining Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) for global exploration and Convex Optimization (CVX) for local refinement. PSO efficiently explores the high-dimensional space, while CVX refines the best candidates within the feasible region. This approach addresses the limitations of gradient descent, which may converge to suboptimal local minima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nVariables:\n- h: A vector of size n_points (100) representing the step function.\n\nObjective:\nMinimize the maximum overlap:\nC\u2085 = max_k [ \u2211_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i]*(1 - h[(i+k) % n_points]) * dx ]\n\nConstraints:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. sum(h) = n_points / 2 (since \u222bh = 1)\n\nPSO Formulation:\n- Position vector: Each particle's position represents a candidate h.\n- Velocity vector: Guides the particle's exploration.\n- Fitness: C\u2085 value of each candidate.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\na. Initialization\n1. If initial_h_values is provided:\n   - Interpolate or adjust to match n_points = 100.\n2. Else:\n   - Initialize as a block function: first half 1.0, second half 0.0.\n\nb. PSO Loop\n1. Initialize particles (e.g., 30 particles) with random positions within constraints.\n2. For each iteration:\n   i. Compute fitness (C\u2085) for all particles.\n   ii. Update personal best and global best.\n   iii. Update velocities: v = w*v + c1*(pbest - current) + c2*(gbest - current).\n   iv. Clip velocities to prevent excessive movement.\n   v. Update positions, then project to [0,1] and normalize to sum = 50.\n3. Terminate on budget_s or lack of improvement.\n\nc. CVX Refinement\n1. Use the best particle as initial guess.\n2. Formulate and solve a convex problem to minimize C\u2085.\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n\nParameters:\n- Particles: 30\n- Inertia (w): 0.7\n- Cognitive (c1): 2.0\n- Social (c2): 2.0\n- Max Iterations: 1000\n\nVariations:\n1. Vary PSO parameters (w, c1, c2) for better exploration.\n2. Try different particle counts to balance exploration and speed.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nTime Estimation:\n- PSO dominates the time, with ~0.1s per iteration for 30 particles.\n- Total ~300s for 1000 iterations, leaving 300s for CVX.\n- Robust handling ensures feasible solutions at each step.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\nFinal Checks:\n1. Ensure sum(h) \u2248 50 and all entries [0,1].\n2. Compute C\u2085 using np.correlate to validate.\n3. Compare with current best and return if improved.",
